Is Enhanced Security (C2) enabled (/usr/sbin/rcmgr get SECURITY BASE)?
According to the removeuser man page (which uses userdel for the actual
delete):

			Security Note
	If you have enhanced security installed on your 
	system, you should use the dxaccounts command 
	to remove users.  See the Security manual and 
	the dxaccounts(8X) reference page for more 
	information.

Also, if you have enhanced security enabled, dxaccounts will only let
you "retire" accounts, not delete (C2 specification, recycling of
UIDs/Usernames not allowed. Man page is slightly misleading). If you
really want to remove an account, the only way will be to do it manually
(as another poster states). From the DUNIX 4.0d docs:

(http://www.unix.digital.com/faqs/publications/pub_page/doc_list.html)

10.4.2    Removing a User Account Manually

	To manually remove a user account from your system: 

	1.Remove the user's files and directories.

	2.Remove the user's entry from the group file.

	3.Remove the user's entry from the passwd file.

	4.Remove the user's /usr/spool/mail/username file.

The rest of that section describes how to do each step. After you remove
the user from the passwd file on an Enhanced Security system, you will
need to run something to get the protected user database in synch. I do
not remember what it is (man prpasswd). Although, as long as the user's
directory/shell definition is gone, that should be enough. I do know the
passwd synch/update is done automagically during reboots... :)

Kinda wish D/T64 UNIX had a "Slightly Enhanced Security" mode.
Basically, shadow passwords with user delete capability... :)

