My company does SQL-DBMS development and support; our product is 
"Adabas D" (distributed by Software AG), and we provide it for 
several Unix ( and Win :-( ) platforms - so that is my background. 
I myself work on DEC (Tru64) and IBM (AIX) platforms. 

The general point for DBMS performance is to avoid disk I/O, 
and that requires RAM. So if you have a large amount of data, 
you need support for large physical (and virtual) homogeneous 
address spaces, this means 64 bit addresses in SW and HW. 
Regardless of CPU performance, there is no substitute for large 
RAM - if you reach these regions, any 64bit machine will 
outperform all 32bit machines just because of RAM cache size. 

In your position I would check availability of (or growth path 
to) multi-CPU configurations with large RAM. Remember that the 
pure number of CPUs is not sufficient, you also need the 
bandwidth of RAM access. (In the AIX newsgroup, Sun seems to have 
a bad reputation for not scaling in this respect ...) 

