After our Compaq reseller installed the same OS patch on both systems the
one ES40 developed a strange problem. While it booted properly and all
srvices started ok no one is able to login to the box.

The box is connected to the network and you can rsh to it and execute
commands and all seems fine. When you telnet, rlogin, or login via the
console tty port you get a message like:

    telnetd: login_tty: Not a typewriter

or   stty: tcgetattr: Operation not supported on socket

The /etc/motd shows up but in an unformatted manner. The newlines are not
be processed properly. The telnet/rlogin then terminates.

The reseller has been looking into the problem but they don't have the
level of unix experience necessary to solve the problem and their best
Unix person has left their company.

I figure the problem must have something to do with the patch causing a
corruption of the tty driver. One thing I tried was to copy over the
/vmunix file from the properly behaving ES40 to the other ES40 and
rebooting. After doing this the system booted but didn't bring up the
ntwork interfaces. It was complaining about a license issue. However one
could log in on the console tty port as root without getting the original
error message about tty not be a typewriter. 

When the support person from the reseller returned to look into the
problem he rebuilt the kernel and booted from it. Now the not a typewriter
error is back but the system boots properly. They are telling me they
think the problem has to do with bad entries in the termcap or terminfo
database but that's crap. So I'm left with trying to resolve this from
across the ocean. I'm getting the serial numbers from the systems and will
call Compaq support early next week but I wanted to canvas this group to
see if anyone has any ideas what to try or have seen similar problems in
the past and can provide some advice.

Any help would be appreciated!

