Mark Koennecke wrote:

> It is in the man pages as nanosleep for DU4.0D. But for some reason I
> call the function as nanosleep_d9.

The "_d9" identifies that version of nanosleep with the long-since
obsolete "draft 9" of the document that eventually (that is, 6 years
ago) became the POSIX 1003.1b-1993 "realtime" standard. You shouldn't be
using that obsolete (and undocumented) function, as it may be removed
"at any time".

The POSIX 1003.1b-1993 nanosleep() function can be found, as someone
already said, in the librt library. You must use -lrt when linking any
program that uses it.

