Mark Koennecke <Mark.Koennecke@psi.ch> writes:

>I have a little  problem here with NFS. All our DU4.0D machines mount a
>filesystem via NFS from a
> a central server. This filesystem holds some generally useful software
>as for instance the GNU stuff.
>Sensible thing to do. The path to that filesystem is in everyones PATH.
>All is fine as long as the server
>is up. As soon as it is down any attempt to run a command, any attempt
>to login will yield endless
>messages of the type NFS server not responding .....

I don't have a good (low cost) suggestion.  (The high cost suggestion
is to get a TruCluster or other highly available server.)

One trick is to put the server path last in the PATH environment
variable, but if you only need a few programs on the server, then
creating symlinks in your bin directory pointing to the executable can
work well, e.g.:

$ ls -l ~/bin | grep contrib
lrwxrwxrwx  ...   20 Mar 18  1996 fig2dev.old -> /contrib/bin/fig2dev
lrwxrwxrwx        21 Mar 18  1996 transfig -> /contrib/bin/transfig
lrwxrwxrwx        17 Mar 18  1996 xfig.v2 -> /contrib/bin/xfig

That way, only when I run xfig do I reach out on the network.

	-Ric Werme
