dblake@popper.ucsf.edu (David T. Blake) writes:
> So that if the environment is not what ksh wants, you get a return
> value of zero. What do I need to do in my program to ensure that
> the above ksh call works properly ?

Have you set WRDE_SHOWERR to see if anything prints to stderr?

> On a second note, it is pretty messed up to have your C library 
> functions rely on ksh expansions. It really ought to go the other
> way.

POSIX Std 1003.2-1992, section E.9.9 (Rationale and Notes):

  While wordexp() could be implemented entirely as a library routine,
  it is expected that most implimentations will run a shell in a
  subprocess to do the expansion.

wordexp() is not even close to what I would consdider a core C library
routine, sh and ksh predate wordexp() considerably, and, given all the
weird parsing they do, getting wordexp() into a form actually usable
by the various shells would be next to impossible.  In context, forking
a shell to do shell word expansion is a reasonable implimentation, as
the POSIX committee acknowledged.
