Subject: Re: Number of file in a directory. Is there a limit ?
Date: 1999/08/25
Message-ID: <7q141s$i5s@ds2.acs.ucalgary.ca>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 517068700
References: <7q0lgc$m5j$1@jaydee.iway.fr> <250819991016365821%harris@zk3.dec.com>
Organization: The University of Calgary
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

Bob Harris (harris@zk3.dec.com) wrote:
: To be specific, there is no limit on the number of regular files. 

While this *may* be formally correct[1], this statement is not particularly
useful. Both ufs and AdvFs use linear arrays of variable-sized directory
entries as the directory structure. This necessarily implies that operations
which do not involve changing (a sufficiently large) directory with N files
(e.g. opening a file) *must* require O(N) time, and will on average examine 
N/2 directory entries. Any operation involving change to the directory (like
creating, removing, or renaming a file) will need to *update* N/2 directory
entries on average. A little table will help to illustrate this:

  Times in seconds on a AdvFS filesystem using DPW-500au with 64M.
  Per-file times are in milliseconds.

    Files     Create[2]         List[3]         rm[4]
            Total  Per-file  Total Per-file   Total Per-file
            (sec)   (ms)     (sec)   (ms)     (sec)   (ms)
   1,000    1.30    1.30      0.08   0.08      1.17   1.17
  10,000   19.94    1.99      0.17   0.02     13.11   1.31
 100,000  885.58    8.86     40.63   0.41    302.04   3.02

  Directories with 1,000 and 10,000 files are still sufficiently small
to fit in the buffer cache, so that per-file performance does not degrade
too much as the directory grows. The 100,000-file directory is too large
to fit into the buffer cache (which has been tuned to 1% of the available
memory, or ca. 600k - while the directory is over 2Mbytes in size), and
all operations involving changing the directory become painfully slow[5].
Even 'ls -1', which is quite speedy for smaller directories now takes
almost a minute ...

  So, while there may be no *theoretical* limit on the number of files
in a single directory (or, at least, the limit is much larger than anything
most people are likely to encounter in practice), handling more that a few
hundred thousands files in a single directory is likely to result in
severely degraded performance.

  Bob, do you plan to introduce hashed directory structures any time soon?

Regards,

/Serge.P

[1] In fact, it is not. From /usr/include/sys/dirent.h, a directory entry
    is at least 12 bytes in size; since AdvFS limits file size to 2**63
    bytes, there is no way to have a directory with more than 0.33*2**61
    entries. The corresponding limit for UFS is 0.33*2**33. In either case,
    since inode number is a 32-bit value, there is no way to have more
    that 2^32-2 *distinct* files in any directory (or filesystem).

[2] time perl -e 'foreach $x(1..100000){open(F,sprintf(">%d",$x))&&close(F)}'
[3] time ls -1 >/dev/null
[4] time sh -c 'find . -type f | xargs rm'
[5] I must emphasize at this point that AdvFS performs *remarkably* well
    on large directories for a file system with linear directory structures.
    I'd seen much worse performance on substantially smaller directories
    on some other unix variants.

--
