You should verify that you can manually send print job over by generating the
appropriate printer driver-codes. For example, in my set up the windows printer
is actually HP5P which is PCL based rather than postscript, So I need to do

	gs -sDEVICE=ljet4 -SOutputFile=tempjob.pcl My_postscipt_file.ps

Or something like that to generate the driver codes. Then I do 

	smbclient \\the_bloody_windows_machine\printer_share -P

plus the appropriate -U (windows user) option and if necessary, type in the 
windows share's password.

Then at the smb prompt, I type "print tempjob.pcl". If things goes well, it would
come out at the printer. If you are luckily to have been blessed with a
postscript printer, you can just generate a little postscript file (latex, xfig,
whatever) and smbclient right away for testing.

Then you can automate this process by setting up a magic filter to do the
gs conversion, setting up your printcap file with an appropriate print queue, and
lastly, use smbprint (a little script from the samba source distribution, I
believe, or from the samba website) as a print filter which drives smbclient 
with some default options. It would be useful for you to read the linux
printing-HOWTO for that particular section on printing to windows printers.

http://www.picante.com/~gtaylor/pht/current/pht.html 


