> Have you set WRDE_SHOWERR to see if anything prints to stderr?

Yes. It fails without error, which is exactly the problem.

 
> > On a second note, it is pretty messed up to have your C library 
> > functions rely on ksh expansions. It really ought to go the other
> > way.
> 
> POSIX Std 1003.2-1992, section E.9.9 (Rationale and Notes):
> 
>   While wordexp() could be implemented entirely as a library routine,
>   it is expected that most implimentations will run a shell in a
>   subprocess to do the expansion.
> 
> wordexp() is not even close to what I would consdider a core C
> library routine, sh and ksh predate wordexp() considerably, and,
> given all the weird parsing they do, getting wordexp() into a
> form actually usable by the various shells would be next to
> impossible. In context, forking a shell to do shell word
> expansion is a reasonable implimentation, as the POSIX committee
> acknowledged.

sh and ksh HAVE to implement the routine in C anyway, or in
whatever language used to contruct them. The GNU C library has a
full implementation of the word expansion, and does NOT fork a
system call to do globbing, tilde expansion, or variable
expansion. About the only thing you NEED the shell for is a
command expansion (ie: such as $(which .Xdefaults) in bash )
Besides, sh doesn't perform full word expansion anyway.

The issue really arises if ksh considers the environment from
which you call wordexp a restricted one, in which case ksh simply
does not do the expansion. That means the C library fails in a 
miserable and confused way leaving you without word expansion
at all, and no errors reported by the routine. 


-- 
