On Tue, 17 Aug 1999 10:39:15 -0400, "Richard Abraham"
<richard.abraham@mnr.gov.on.ca> wrote:

>1.  Why is the nsr installation instructing that the index file be skipped.

Because Networker backs up the indexes separately, when backup of all
other file systems is finished. In this way, the index  on tape
contains also the info of the file systems on the same tape.

>2.  The only information I can get about recovering the index file refers
>      to a bootstrap save.  How do I do a bootstrap save and do I have
>      to or is it safe to take the .nsr file out of the
>      /var/nsr/index/<machine name>/ directory?

With the default configuration, Networker mails a report to root. In
this report the backup status of all the file systems is reported.
This report should also contain two lines mentioning the indexes and
the bootstrap.

Make sure you test your recover strategy before having to rely on your
backup! Read nsr_crash(8). If you only have a Networker backup, you
have to re-install UNIX and Networker from CD before reloading your
Networker tape! Good scenario if you have the time (and the CDs and
license info present ;-)  It might be faster to have a vdump tape with
/ and /usr to get Networker running.

>Thanks in advance.
>
>Richard Abraham

