<Mark.Koennecke@psi.ch> wrote:

>I have a little  problem here with NFS. All our DU4.0D machines mount a
>filesystem via NFS from a
> a central server. This filesystem holds some generally useful software
>as for instance the GNU stuff.
>Sensible thing to do. The path to that filesystem is in everyones PATH.
>All is fine as long as the server
>is up. As soon as it is down any attempt to run a command, any attempt
>to login will yield endless
>messages of the type NFS server not responding ..... This renders the
>machine almost unusable though
>otherwise it would run just fine just without the extra software.  In
>that stage I cannot unmount the NFS
>filesystem either as it is says it is busy.  The NFS option soft is
>already active.
>
>Has anybody got a clue how to resolve this? The ideal behaviour would be
>when the machine just
>complains  a few times when the NFS filesystem is accessed but continue
>working otherwise.

I read the mount man page and it looks like the 'soft' option may be
what you want:

mount -o soft

        soft - returns an error if the server does not respond

/etc/fstab

        node:/path  /mount_point  nfs ro,bg,soft 0 0

However, I've never played with it, so I don't know if it will do what
you want or not.



