In comp.unix.osf.osf1 David Bremner <david_bremner@yahoo.com> wrote:
> peter@baileynm.com (Peter da Silva) writes:

>> 
>> I'm not sure what a hobbyist would want to do with Tru64 that wouldn't
>> be better done by one of the free BSD distributions, all of which run on
>> the Alpha now. Tru64 UNIX is basically a commercialized BSD anyway.
>> 

> Unless the compilers are included.  And atom. That would be attractive
> to the eccentrics that like to compute things :-)

Don't forget that Tru64 also has one of the fastest Java implementations.
They got both JDK 1.1.7 and JDK 1.2.1 as well as a rather fast JIT
compiler for JDK 1.1.7. They apparently put a lot of efford into these
products and better yet: They can be had for free from www.digital.com/java.

This makes Tru64 a rather decent development platform for Java stuff
as well as a fast Web server for servlets! JDK 1.2 support for Linux
(not to mention Linux/AXP) is lagging behing a lot.

I think it'll take a rather long time until we see something like that
for Linux/AXP or NetBSD. 

So: Go for it. Let's ask them for a Tru64 hobbist license!!

Tom
