They would get an operating system that installs right the first time...

Actually, this is probably due to my own inexperience. I just started
playing with NetBSD/i386 last week. So far, I have watched the NetBSD
install hang, "locked on myself", and panic before finally getting a
clean install. I've had less trouble upgrading DUNIX 3.2c to 4.0b than
with this. Then again, it probably dosen't help that I'm installing
using an "alternate" method (MSDOS partition either on separate or same
drive). I'd do an FTP install, except the networks cards in the machines
are some of the few NOT supported by the base install floppy kernel
(DE205). Does NetBSD/Alpha have this many problems? 

If you need some more, NetBSD/Alpha does not officially support multiple
processors (http://www.netbsd.org/Ports/alpha/models.html). The Linux
folks recently (relatively speaking) added multiple processor support.
Within the past few months, it was optimized to a "useable" level
(finally reaching and beating Microsoft performance levels). Not to
mention DUNIX/Tru64 comes with a decent GUI. Or at least my NetBSD/i386
only came with the base XF86. Does NetBSD/Alpha get anything different?

Incidently, FreeBSD is "an advanced BSD UNIX operating system for
"PC-compatible" computers" (eg. x86 only. No ports. Sorry).
OpenBSD/Alpha does not officially support "AlphaServer" calibur systems
or multiple processors (http://www.openbsd.org/alpha.html). The same URL
also states that the Alpha port is not "actively" updated (point to
NetBSD. They've been doing quite a bit a work).

Now to be fair, I'll give NetBSD another point for being able to run on
VAX and Alpha. I'd give one to Linux too, but their VAX port is still
considered very beta.

Personally, even at $100 fee, a hobbyist Tru64 UNIX which is
commercially supported and was designed for Alpha systems from day one
is looking like a good deal.

-- 

