I'm sorry that I confused the issue of NetBSD and FreeBSD.
That's just own ignorance of the open Unix community. From
a DECUS perspective, the question is not which one  - but who
wants to participate. In this regard, DECUS provides the
forum - it's up to members (you guys) to flesh it out with
the areas of your interest.

Relative to the question of Usenix versus DECUS - it's
clear that Usenix takes the middle road - the pure light
path of Unix. There's a lot to be said for that and for those
that are pursuing the common and comparative ground of
Unix.

But sometimes there is a need for the specifics - how to
leverage the maximum from your systems. A place where
you can participate in helping to shape the directions
of those system in terms of the futures you see. That can
only really happen at the h/w ~ o/s vendor's forum, where the
engineers that make this stuff can pay attention to you more
so than their competitors across the aisle.

It is a powerful construct and it has worked very well for those
that have taken advantage of it (and maintained their support of it).
The VMS community is a great example of that.

So the goal is to try and get something similar going for the Compaq
Unix
community. My own impressions are that a lively vocal community
is what has kept VMS alive and thriving through all these transitions.
With the corollary that without some active voices in the Compaq
Unix space - the Compaq higher-ups simply listen to the overly select
few and not hear the voices of those in trenches that live and
breath this on a daily basis..

I will gladly confess I'm no purist here. We (the Space Telescope
Science Institute) have made a major commitment to Alpha's running
Unix (Tru64) and I want to see that flourish. And my past experiences
with VMS and its community shows me this can be a very effective
way to see that this system delivers the goods - now and in the future.

 - joe

pollizzi@stsci.edu




