My understanding is dop is the Xwindows equivalent to su. Typically, it
will automatically pop up when someone tries to do something that
requires root privileges, which is specified in /etc/doprc. Just like
su, one needs to know the root password in order to use it. Beyond that,
all I get from dop is:

Usage:
        dop [name args] |
            [-a application-name] |
            [-d application-name]
where:
            -a adds a new entry to the doprc file
            -d deletes an entry from the doprc file
Note: application-name should contain the full path.

I have not seen a mention of it in man pages or doc sets (I must not be
looking hard enough).

Also, what version of DUNIX is being used? If 4.0-4.0b, then take a look
at:

	http://www.cert.org/ftp/cert_bulletins/VB-97.01.dec

Then you might want to explain some of the cool features of sudo (like
unauthorized attempt notification).
-- 

