F. Flintstone <blah@blob.com> wrote:

>needs.  I'm running a TSP program which requires 450MB but it never
>completes successfully and indicates that it could only aquire 130MB

Maybe you have your ulimit set to the amount of physical RAM? Try
setting it to unlimited (man ulimit). Another possibility is, that
your system wide kernel settings limit the amount of memory.

Try launching dxkerneltuner and take a look at the proc -category.
There are a number of variables there, which control memory allocation.
Example: max-per-proc-address-space. Also, under category ipc there are
more of them controlling the shared memory allocation.

You can also list/set these with sysconfig from command line or edit
/etc/sysconfigtab and add them there; next reboot will take them in
effect.

>with 128MB of physical ram.  I 'm not sure how much virtual mem I
>have....and how do I check?  I used Performance manager to watch free

swapon -s gives you swap space information. vmstat and top (if you have
it installed) give information about memory usage.


