I've sent this message to Joe (with some added flames which have been removed here)



Your post is a great way to get the ball rolling and has some great
ideas.

Questions: Who would give the sessions ?
           How would DECUS find appropriate volunteers ?
 

I don't believe that Compaq is  in a position to give many of
the sessions you suggest.  Technical product sessions yes, but
comparison, gotcha etc no.  In my opinion, these have to come from users.
And more importantly from different users so DECUS attendees can
see many different sides of each story.


Also, I don't think the low(Unix) attendence is because of lack of sessions.
As you say, there were many great Unix sessions in Providence. I think
we don't know why there is low Unix attendence.  I think we
are making the assumption that people are attending other
conferences but maybe not.  We need to know why they
do not attend the DECUS symposium.  Perhaps its lack of PR.  Adding
great sessions without addressing this problem won't help.

I think we need to differentiate DECUS and the symposium from other
events.  And we need to advertise and let the Compaq TRU64 folks know
that this is a TRU64 event. Are we sending DECUS info to every TRU64 site ?
Are we following up ?  Are we letting them know that they can have input ?
(can they have input ????)


I have some suggestions that need some brainstorming

     A TRU64 newsletter (with events, discussions etc). On line AND
      hardcopy.  Articles such as my experience with field test of
      TRU64 5.0 and the new TRUcluster would I believe generate
      interest. This would SHOW people what DECUS is all about and
      what they might expect at the symposium.  Telling them is one
      thing, showing is another

     Find out who are the TRU64 users.  Ask if they would
     like to subscribe to the  TRUcluster WG mailing list or Unix Sig
     mailing list.  Make sure that these lists are active and vital.
     We need to generate enthusiasm and then people will want to
     attend the symposium.

     I think Bill Magill has some good ideas for the web and booklets.
     I think these sould be adopted and enhanced.  More importantely
     I think we need to get volunteers to work on this stuff.
     Again, we would be showing people that they can participate.
     As to specific sessions:

For me specifically

  None of the keynote type sessions you mentioned interest me.
  (but then again none in Providence did either).


For the technical sessions
 >> A session that would help to focus/position TRU64 against other Unix

   This is tough.  It's not clear that any of us know that much about
    other Unixes (try to compare TRUcluster with HACMP and see who knows
     what)

    However several session on what people are doing with TRU64 and TRUcluster
    that may differentiate TRU64 use from others would allow people to
    come to their own conclusions.

    What I think would be useful for TRU64 people to see
       sites that are using Enhanced Security
                            ACLs
                            TRUcluster (in lots of different environments)
                            Dataless
                            non-linux batch farms
                      etc
    In my opinion what we want to do is show people how they can better
    use their
    TRU64 and how it will make their computing environment better.
    We need to show by example.


>>Case Study sessions
   great !

>> Beowulf
     great but I'd also like to see a comparison using TRU64 and dataless
       cost, reliability etc

>> Linux
     great  but I'd like to see comparisons: upside but also the downside



Enough ramblings. Can we get a dialogue going ?  I'd like to
get the TRUcluster working group up and active.  Any suggestions as
to how to give this a push would be most welcome (perhaps some pr
on the decus.org web server ?)


Thanks
and regards,
Rochelle



