Compaq/DECUS can't forget that they have their own Unix OS, an award-winning
system that many of us like quite a bit.  Don't fall victim totally to the
Linux hype machine.

I've been a Linux supporter for years, but things have gotten a little out
of hands on the Linux hype front.  I'd dearly love to see some DECUS
(and general user community) emphasis on Tru64.  For goodness sakes, this OS
has won numerous awards, has been favored in the top 2 or 3 Unix-variants by
numerous analyst firms and yet there are still times when I feel like i've
worked with one of the worlds best-kept secrets.  It took Digital a while to
get Tru64 to this point, and yet it seems like it doesn't get the attention
or for that matter devotion that Sun does (who has had just as many problems
if no more in the past with Solaris, an OS effort launch in similar
time-frame to OSF/1)!

The end result of this has been ISVs starting to reconsider their plans. 
The company I currently work for (and of course, who does not share my
opinions and I'm not authorized to speak for...) has since dropped support
for both Alpha NT & Tru64.  Much of this is because many of our partner ISVs
either have not or do not support Tru4 themselves, making it difficult for
our corporation to support the comprehensive engineering solutions they'd
like to offer.  Not to say that I'm happy or agree with the decisions
they've made, but thats the way things lie. 

I have long suspected that much of this decision is due to the almost
non-existent standing that Compaq has made on the issue of technical
computing.  Sure, they're out the trumpeting the Alpha processor, but in the
form of AlphaServers.  The XP1000 recently reclaimed the top spot on the
SPEC list, and yet the newsfront is quiet.  We've got affordable workstation
solutions available, and they even dual-support NT and Unix, and yet they
can't capture the market from people like HP and Sun.  You know, HP, the
vendor who releases a new C-class workstation (the 360) and claims its the
fastest on the market merely because it's faster than their previous
offering which at one point in time was fairly quick.  And Sun, who seems at
times like they can't decide whether they're in the workstation business
either (witness the amazing holes in their current product line, the
enormous gaps in performance they've suffered over the years, etc.).

The sad part of this for me is that my own company has fallen into the
NT-Intel movement.  Unigraphics can trace it's history back to its early
days first on Data General hardware and then for the longest time on VMS. 
They made the move from VMS only a couple of years (and only 1 release) ago,
with promises of support on either Alpha NT or Unix, and yet now they have
decided to drop support, in my personal opinion, due to lack of clear vision
and interest from Compaq.

I hope we can get some interest in the technical computing arena, especially
in the engineering areas, in the DECUS group to help keep this marvelous
platform going.  Before I signed on to my current employer to act as a
consulting systems engineer, I worked for an all-Alpha Unigraphics shop.  I
heard recently that prior to the Alpha announcement from UG, they had done
some UG benchmarking on the initial XP1000s versus Pentium IIs, Xeons, IIIs,
HP PA-8500s, and the like, to find that they got the best numbers
(especially in terms of bang-for-the-buck) on XP1000s.  Too bad they won't
have the opportunity to go that route now, as they try to determine where to
move 80+ seats of Digital Unix on DEC 3000s, AlphaStations, and PWS 600aus
to.  They've already switched a dozen or so seats to HP C360s, and the rest
appear to be leaning towards Compaq AP500 Pentium IIs running NT.  Unix lost
out in this case...Only 6 months after a highly pitched battle where yours
truly and a few other "Tru" believers had successfully waged war against a
user-management who desperately wanted NT boxes everywhere so they could run
Solitaire at lunchtime (thats the most noble motivation i can credit them
with in that fight...).

: Enough ramblings. Can we get a dialogue going ?  I'd like to
