In article <37b4da88.25644054@news.rdc1.bc.wave.home.com>, Scott
Ritchie <s.ritchie@home.com> wrote:

>On Fri, 13 Aug 1999 20:10:01 GMT, stu@c49395-a.wodhvn1.mi.home.com
>(Stuart R. Fuller) wrote:
...
>..................................  So I took out all of the memory
>and had a visual inspection.  Again everything looked fine.  I put it
>(8 moduals) back in and booted the memory reported 55.4 MB available.
>(this what it should be).  It seems that the problem is solved, or is
>it?  Could it be the memory is on the edge of becoming deceased?  

This is a guess, but there is a way to tell the system that there is
less real memory then is physically present (memlimit).  I think that
once this is set, you need to power cycle the system to reset it.

I'm guessing that maybe when you started the CD-ROM boot to re-install,
and then aborted that operation, that maybe the CD-ROM booting code had
set a 32MB memory limit for some strange reason.

If the first time you turned off the power was to pull the memory SIMMs
and plug them back in, that may have been when the memlimit was reset.

Again, this is all wild speculation on my part.  I just figured I would
mention it so that you don't go around thinking your memory is about to
die.  I don't think memory that is border line would just drop out in a
32MB increment.  Memory tends to generate parity errors, not just drop
out.

Good luck, and I'm glad you got the use of all your memory back.



