In article <37A348C8.133816C1@salud.unm.edu>, <dminor@salud.unm.edu>
wrote:

>Hi. We had a box running 3.2c with several advfs vols on it. Yesterday
>we installed 4.0e on a new disk in the system and are in the process of
>migrating files, etc.
>
>So far none of our advfs vols is showing up in the new setup. None of
>the advfs structure has changed. On boot you can see them scroll by with
>the message "no device." We did copy over the /etc/fdmns file which
>seems to be correct. Any suggestions?? This is kind of an emergency
>because all of our core stuff is on those vols.

Since you installed v4.0e on a new disk, it doesn't have the /etc/fdmns
entries for the AdvFS domains.

2nd, it is recommended that you run the /sbin/advfs/verify utility
against your AdvFS domains before you mount them on v4.0e.  (man 8
verify).  This is to clean up any problems that the v4.0e kernel might
panic over (this is actually a v4.0d recommendation, but it would apply
to v4.0e when using AdvFS domains created on a prior release.

If you look at your old system disk you will see that the /etc/fdmns
directory contains subdirectories named for each of your AdvFS domains. 
Inside of each of those subdirectories are symbolic links to the
/dev/rzXX devices that make up the volumes in each domain.

You can manually recreate these /etc/fdmns subdirectories and symbolic
links on your v4.0e system or you can use the /sbin/advfs/advscan
utility to do it for you.  (man 8 advscan)

After that you should be able to mount your AdvFS domains on v4.0e.



