For some time now, we (DECUS) have been trying
to broaden the audience of our symposium to include
those interested in both Unix and NT, as well as the
established VMS crowd. While we've made some
progress in addressing the NT area - we are still
very short in reaching Compaq's Unix customers.

It's rather unfortunate too. At our recent Providence
meeting, Compaq really put on a bang-up show with
a number of very interesting technical sessions on Tru64,
TruCluster and Linux. And to back up what they had
in sessions, they brought with them (in the exhibit area)
a Beowulf cluster (a rack of DS10's) and a 4-cpu
SMP ES40 running Linux, in addition to their Tru64
systems. While the turn-out wasn't bad for the VMS and
NT sessions - there was a rather low draw for the
Unix ones.

So what gives?

Yes, we know there are a number of other Unix specific
events, and even those already dedicated to Linux.

Our goal isn't to compete with a generic approach
to these operating systems - but to focus on the unique
aspects of how to leverage the most from Alpha's running
Tru64 and Alpha's and Compaq's Intel servers running Linux.
Where else can you really get the full attention of
the Compaq engineers that build this stuff for you
then in an environment that's dedicated to them?

But we're still not getting your attention.

So, banging our heads together with our Compaq Unix
colleagues we've thought of a couple of topics, sessions
keynotes ("Grabber sessions" if you will) that might
attract your interest and give you that needed edge to
come to our event. But before we go investing in 1000
different directions, we thought it might be a good idea
just to test some samples against you.

Let us know if we're heading in the right direction
or what other kinds of information/activities/...
you'd look for to justify your investment of coming
to our symposium for Compaq's Unix info.

The following are in no particular order - nor anything
more than just some ideas - but we'd like your feedback:

Potential Grabber (Technical Keynote type) Sessions:

   - E-Bay Done Right - How do you really configure
     an e-commerce system to avoid the problems that
     have plagued E-Bay and other notable e-commerce
     sites.

   - Oscar Winning Computing - how artistry and high
     performance computing  come together
     to create the stunning images we now find in the
     entertainment industry.

   - How I Put Up a Top-Ten Web Site and Survived -
     Case study in how a major international site planned,
     deployed, and then scaled their systems to meet
     the now typical flood of hits.

   - Advances In Supercomputing with Commercial
     Off-The-Shelf Systems - The advances being made
     by using clustering of inexpensive systems and
     samples of the kinds of problems these systems
     are now addressing.

   - Next Generation I/P Telephony Systems - with the
     rapid availability of low cost but high performance
     computing - what new directions can be taken in
     how we communicate.

   - Virtual Prototyping in the Automotive or Drug Industries-
     How the automotive engineers and/or pharmacologists use
     the new technologies to visualize/evaluate the next
     generations of their products without having to physically
     build them.

Technical type sessions - but a little different:

   - Something's different about Tru64 - A session that
     would help to focus/position Tru64 against other Unix
     implementations.

   - How I got real solutions using this stuff - case study
     type session(s).

   - What really is clustering v.s. linear scalable computing -
     how to read between the hype. A true comparison between
     SMP & clustering and shared memory architectures.

   - Meet the engineers - standard fare for our event,
     but perhaps you didn't know. This kind of session
     really takes the fetters off the engineers and lets
     them converse with you about the problems you see,
     what works, what doesn't, where you and they are heading...

   - Open the Kimono - short vignettes of individual experiences
     of how they survived with Tru64, wrinkles, warts and all.

   - Living on the boundary of Linux and Tru64 - when to use which,
     when to use both, and how to leverage the maximum advantage of
each.

   - E-Business - A Dirty Little Secret - making a successful
     e-commerce business is not just about deploying the right
     kind of hardware.

   - What JAVA Is and Isn't - Another dirty little secrets type
     session. What are the kinks in Java to be aware of and how
     to be most successful with it.

   - "Beowulf," What Is It and Why Should I Care ? - talk on
     clustering approaches with Linux.

   - What Is This Linux/"Open Source" Thing All  About? - more
     than just a new & inexpensive operating system, Linux is also
     advancing the whole genre of Open Source Computing. What is
     this genre, and what are some of the implications for the
     industry?

   - The World's Fastest JAVA Machine - as a friend of mine says, "if
     ya got'em - smoke'em" A talk about the java implementations on
     Alpha

   - Glueware - Where Is It At - Where Is It Going? - Middleware
     was the hew and cry of just a few years ago. What is the state
     of the art/practice and what are the practical results from
     this technology -  specific to the Unix community.

   - Tru64 Issues on IA64 - A peek into the issues and directions
     of deploying Tru64 on the new Intel architecture.

   - IA64 The Technical Story - similar to the above, but focusing
     on the IA64 architecture itself.


Again, no promises that we'd do any of the above - but we'd
at least like to know which of these (or other) kinds of
sessions/activities would be of most interest to the Compaq
Unix community. Please either respond in this notes conference
or e-mail me at pollizzi@stsci.edu


Thanks,

 - joe pollizzi








