F. Flintstone wrote:
> 
>   I used to be able to log in as root with X-win32 to the DEC server
> running Unix 0.4d.  But now when I type try it it says "not on
> console".  I have no idea why I can no longer log in as root from a
> machine that is not the server.  Help!  Thanks.

First, allowing direct root logins from anywhere except the console is a
bad idea. Use of su and/or the X equivalent (which usually pops up
automagically when root-type privileges are needed) is usually a better
idea. Unless, of course, you are also on a college campus with an open
network where students run sniffers, at which point adopting a "no
clear-text root accessible passwords" policy is a good idea. With that
said...

Have you made any changes recently, along the lines of setting up
"Enhanced Security", patches, or upgrades?

In any case, /etc/securettys lists the hosts/teminal lines/etc where
direct root logins are allowed. By default it will only have
/dev/console and :0. I added /dev/tty00 for a directly connected VT420
within 3 feet of the machine. You will need to add the display name of
the Xserver you are wanting to login from (kept in /etc/auth/system/ttys
??).

More info can be found in man securettys or in the online DUNIX Docs
under the "System Administration" section.
-- 
Until soon...
Ed Wensell III



