From: Barry Andreasen <bja@zk3.dec.com>
Subject: Re: libcurses and patches for 4.0
Date: 1999/08/10
Message-ID: <37B08BFD.8B7E2E60@zk3.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 511504566
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
Organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Mime-Version: 1.0
Reply-To: bja@zk3.dec.com
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

After working with Jon on this we have isolated the problem and I
thought that posting the results would be beneficial to other developers
as well.  I tried to post this reply yesterday, but somehow I goofed
since I don't see it, but if this is a second, please excuse the
redundancy.

In debugging the application Jon mentioned, it was found that the segv
was occurring in the application code, when it tried to write to an
invalid address.  Further investigation found that address was expected
to be to a global application variable, but the variable involved was
also declared as a global variable with a similar definition in the
curses library.  While the application source code included a definition
for the pointer, the linker and loader had resolved the address to be in
the library memory allocation.

After some research we learned that during compilation, the linker
categorizes each variable.  In the general case, the linker, when it
finds new declarations will allocate space in the local memory region
and categorize the variable as an 'allocated common' (odump -Dtv
libcurses.so).  When the curses library was built, this variable was
unique and therefore generated an allocated region and became a GLOBAL
ACOMM.  However, when the linker finds an existing entry for a new
variable (from a library), it does not do the allocation, and enters the
variable as a simple common (COMM) and uses the offset to the existing
allocated space from the ACOMM variable (odump -Dgv <app.obj>).

Previous versions of the libcurses.so ignored this violation, and
fortunately functioned correctly, but the updated library versions
blocked this violation and uncovered the memory overwrite, causing the
application to segv.

Since the specification for the curses interface indicates that certain
variable names are reserved for the library implementation (this
variable falls into that category), the solution is rename the
application variable or force the local application declaration to do an
allocation in the application data space by including an assignment in
the declaration
 i.e. char *BC=NULL;


This will cause the linker to classify the variable as DATA and place it
in the application DATA space.  When the loader encounters the variable,
as data, it will use the local memory storage instead of writing to the
library memory area.
-b

