"Kim, Hyunchul" wrote:

> I'm writing an application which traces system calls of target processes.
>
> My problem is that when a target process do 'execve' to execute a set-uid
> program
> (with uid 0), the process comes to stop.
>
> Then, neither getting the status of the process with
>     'ioctl(procfd, PIOCSTATUS, &prstatus)'
> or making it running with
>     'ioctl(procfd, PIOCRUN, NULL)
> succeeds.
> The 'errno' is EWOULDBLOCK(EAGAIN).
>
> Can I trace a root-set-uid program?
> If I can, how can i do?

You should get an error return from the PIOCWSTOP
ioctl (the EAGAIN you're seeing -- same as EWOULDBLOCK).
When this happens, you need to reopen the process again to get
a new file descriptor and only then close the current and now invalid
tracing fd.

The process is actually stopped waiting for you to do this. If you
just close in invalid fd, the setuid process will continue running
detached from your trace.

Hope I remember this correctly -- it's been a long time. It should have
been in the man page for proc, which admittedly is pretty much a
disaster.

-- Jeff
